sandbox selftest, Seatbelt ENFORCED here: the claims below are measured against the kernel
  OK   the tilde case is not the confinement case: open('~/.ssh/x') writes into the root, because open() never expands a tilde
  OK   reap_own_artifact deletes our own leftover...
  OK   ...and refuses anything else: a sweep of ~/.ssh that deleted a real key would be the worse bug
  OK   ...and is quiet about a file that was never there
  OK   profile double-quotes the root: a single-quoted path is read as a SYMBOL and sandbox-exec dies with 'unexpected symbol argument'
  OK   profile is given the REALPATH: /var/folders is a symlink to /private/var/folders and the exception silently misses the symlink
  OK   profile shape: one blanket write-deny AND one filtered allow naming this root — the two lines the clause's 'writable root' rests on (specificity, not order, is what binds: see `profile`'s docstring)
  OK   (deny network-outbound) is in the profile every seam receives
  OK   a box with no wrapper degrades to no prefix and still verifies (a Linux rollout host is not broken by this module)
  OK   seatbelt() is an enforcement probe, not a Path.exists(): this box has the wrapper AND a deny-only profile really refuses a write
  OK   a wrapper that IGNORES its profile yields seatbelt()=False and an empty prefix — no check here can then pass unearned, which is the only honest failure mode left
  OK   VECTOR: a candidate that writes to ~/.ssh is refused by the kernel, not by us pattern-matching the path
  OK   ...and the file it tried to create still does not exist afterwards
  OK   VECTOR: a candidate that opens a socket is refused by the kernel before a packet leaves
  OK   the refusal is not only the raw syscall: urllib raises too, and the harness reports it as a verdict (a candidate cannot hide behind its own try/except because the ERROR line is what the retry sees)
  OK   and a HOSTNAME never resolves either — name service is itself outbound, so the refusal arrives early as gaierror, not as a long timeout
  OK   VECTOR: the suite still RANKS a hostile candidate like any partial answer — the assert before the refusal passed, so passed/total say 1/2
  OK   ...and the retry loop still gets actionable feedback about it, in the same GOT/WANT/ERROR shape every other failure uses
  OK   no hostile candidate hung or crashed the harness: the verdicts above came back in one run each
  OK   a benign candidate verifies exactly as it did without the sandbox
  OK   TMPDIR is retargeted into the root, so a candidate's own temp files land in the directory we hand it and delete after it
  OK   the root may reach us in the /var symlink form and the write exception still binds, because profile() realpaths it
  OK   a relative write lands in the root and the candidate's cwd IS the root — without that, 'writable root' would not confine the common case
  OK   a candidate that shells out keeps working, and its child inherits the same confinement (the null device is the profile's one exception)
  OK   reads stay open, and that is a STATED limit of this sandbox, not an accident: the clause asks for a writable root, no network and rlimits
  OK   RLIMIT_CPU binds THROUGH the wrapper: a busy loop died on the signal in about its cpu seconds, not at the 30s wall timeout
  OK   the child reports the limit it was given (witness from inside the sandbox, not a number printed from outside it)
  OK   the default cpu budget is wider than the wall clock by the box's own width, so a candidate that fans out workers is not killed for our arithmetic (measured: the wall timeout is what actually fires)
  OK   RLIMIT_FSIZE turns a disk-filling write into an ordinary failure: the kernel stops the writer — whether this platform surfaces EFBIG to the interpreter (measured here on macOS) or kills it with SIGXFSZ (Linux) — and either way the file stays under the cap
  OK   memory ceiling is what a fresh child answers after TRYING setrlimit, so the claim tracks the platform instead of a table here
  OK   status names every layer the clause asks for, including the one this box cannot do
  OK   the five candidate-execution seams — harness run_test and _probes, debug _run, confidence _seeded_run and edge_probe — spawn through sandbox.run, so the clause is one call site and not a convention
  OK   ...and each one is given a root of its own, not the caller's cwd
  OK   a multi-file set still imports its sibling module from inside the sandbox: the <TMPDIR> bootstrap and cwd=root agree, so ranking survives the move (1/2, not an error-out)

